        <title>Music with Kafka</title>
        <published>2018-04-18T00:00:00+00:00</published>
        <updated>2018-04-18T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
        <author>
          <name>Unknown</name>
        </author>
        <link rel="alternate" href="https://imflog.github.io/talks/music-kafka/" type="text/html"/>
        <id>https://imflog.github.io/talks/music-kafka/</id>
        
        <content type="html">&lt;p&gt;In this long conference (3 hours), with my friends &lt;a href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.linkedin.com&#x2F;in&#x2F;vivien-maleze-1635b094&#x2F;&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;Vivien Maleze&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;
and &lt;a href=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.linkedin.com&#x2F;in&#x2F;paul-boutes-611831a9&#x2F;&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;Paul Boutes&lt;&#x2F;a&gt;, we explained all the concept useful when working with Kafka.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To put this in practice we created a live application that plays and mix samples requested by the audience using Tweets.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
